    "q-94176": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94176",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}$ in terms of the components of $y.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\ngives the following expressions for ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy solving the second equation for ${x}_{2}$ and substituting it into the first equation, we obtain the following expressions for ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}-2({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})(-{y}_{2})-4(-{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-4{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
